  "easy": {
    "heading": "Starting the Morning with a Quick Win",
    "body": "I hope you have your coffee ready because today's easy puzzle was a real treat. I started by looking for the smallest numbers since they usually give away the most secrets. The sum region at 1,3 with a target of 1 was my first stop. Since that region is just one single cell, I knew that spot had to be a 1. I looked at our domino list and saw the 4,1 tile, so I placed it there, connecting 2,3 and 1,3. This was a great start because it immediately told me that cell 2,3 was a 4.\n\nFrom there, things just started falling into place like a row of, well, dominoes. The equals region at 2,3 and 3,3 meant that cell 3,3 also had to be a 4. I paired it with the 4,2 domino in the 3,3 and 3,2 spots. That gave me a 2 in cell 3,2, which helped me satisfy the equals region at 3,1 and 3,2. I ended up using the 2,0 tile there, putting the 2 in 3,1 and the 0 in 2,1. The whole thing finished up really smoothly once those big equals constraints were out of the way.",
  },
  "medium": {
    "heading": "A Little Midday Brain Teaser",
    "body": "The medium puzzle today definitely stepped things up a notch. The thing that caught my eye first was the long equals region across 3,0, 3,1, and 3,2. Having three cells in a row that all must be the same number is usually a huge hint. I looked at the tiles and realized the only way to make this work was to use the 6,6 domino for cells 3,0 and 3,1. This forced cell 3,2 to also be a 6. Since cell 4,2 had a greater than 1 constraint, I used the 5,6 domino to connect 4,2 and 3,2, putting the 5 in cell 4,2. \n\nAnother tricky spot was the sum of 7 at 0,1 and 1,1. I had to be careful here because there were a few ways to get to 7, but looking at the surrounding equals constraints helped narrow it down. I eventually placed the 0,3 domino at 0,2 and 0,1, which meant cell 0,2 was 0 and cell 0,1 was 3. This left cell 1,1 needing to be a 4 to finish that sum of 7. It took a minute to visualize how the tiles would twist around the board, but once I got that 6,6 tile placed, the rest felt much more manageable.",
  },
  "hard": {
    "heading": "The Late Night Logic Challenge",
    "body": "Wow, the hard puzzle today really made me work for it! The biggest hurdle was that huge less than 2 region covering four different cells. That essentially meant those cells had to be mostly 0s and maybe a 1. I spent a good while staring at the bottom of the board first, though, specifically the sum of 8 and sum of 4 regions. I used the 6,0 domino for cells 5,0 and 6,0, and then used the 6,3 tile for 6,3 and 6,4. This helped clear out some of the larger numbers so I could focus on the tight constraints at the top.\n\nThere was a moment where I got stuck near the middle, but the sum of 2 at 2,0 and 3,0 was the breakthrough. By placing the 2,1 tile there, it forced the 2,0 tile into the 2,0 and 1,0 spots. This created a chain reaction up to the top left corner. The very last piece I placed was the 0,1 domino at 0,1 and 0,0, which perfectly satisfied the sum of 2 region. It felt so satisfying to see the whole grid filled up after that initial struggle with the low-value zones.",
  },
  "tips": "Try to look for regions that only have one cell first, as those are essentially free numbers. Always check your sum regions before placing a domino that uses a high number like 6, as those can quickly exceed your target. If you get stuck, look for the equals constraints because they limit your options more than you might think.",
  "learned": "Today I realized how much the empty cells can change the flow of the puzzle. In the hard grid, those gaps really forced me to take longer paths with my dominoes than I expected. I also learned that when you have a less than constraint covering many cells, you should save your 0 and 1 tiles for that area specifically.",
